1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:16,600
Welcome to this iOS development course. Over eleven sessions you'll go from not having
written a line of Swift to being able to design, build, test and document an iOS application
of your own.

2
00:00:16,600 --> 00:00:22,600
Here is what you will be able to do by the end of this video.

3
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:30,200
The course is built in two halves. The first seven modules are the technical curriculum, and
they climb deliberately.

4
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:54,200
Module one is the language and the tools. Module two adds the interface layer. Modules
three, four and five layer on the language features, the networking and the storage that
turn a screen into a real app. Module six is architecture — how to keep it all maintainable.

5
00:00:54,200 --> 00:01:14,200
Then three craft chapters that no single API teaches you. A case study that assembles
everything into one product. A chapter on testing. And a chapter on documentation — which
sounds like the boring one, and is the one that decides whether your work survives being

6
00:01:14,200 --> 00:01:25,400
Every module ends with a practice exercise. Do them. Watching someone else write code feels
like learning, but it isn't — the exercises are where it becomes yours.

7
00:01:25,400 --> 00:01:43,400
What you need. A Mac — that one isn't negotiable, iOS development requires macOS. Xcode,
which is free from the Mac App Store and includes everything: the editor, the visual
interface builder, the simulator and the debugger. And a free Apple account to download it.

8
00:01:43,400 --> 00:01:56,600
You don't need a physical iPhone to follow along. The simulator covers almost everything.
You will need a real device eventually for anything touching the camera or the sensors, but
not to learn.

9
00:01:56,600 --> 00:02:17,400
A word on how the example app is used. Rather than teach each concept with a throwaway
snippet, every module shows the idea twice — once in isolation, and once inside a real,
working application, so you see what it looks like when it has to survive contact with an

10
00:02:17,400 --> 00:02:39,000
The app happens to be a ticket booker, but nothing about it is special. Around a hundred and
thirty seven Swift files, no third-party libraries, running entirely on the device with no
backend — which makes it good for teaching, because every layer is visible. Any reasonably

11
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:48,600
So the useful question isn't "how do I build this screen?" It's "where does this idea fit in
the thing I want to make?"

12
00:02:48,600 --> 00:02:51,100
Let's recap the key points.

13
00:02:51,100 --> 00:03:01,500
In Module one we install the tools, create a first project, and cover the Swift language
foundations that everything else is built on. See you there.
